      "instructions": "1-6: Complete the parcel declaration. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n7-10: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Callum Webb: Good morning. Are you sending an international parcel today, or do you first need advice about the declaration?\nMoana Reed: Both, please. The parcel is for my cousin in Vancouver. I completed part of the online form last night, but I stopped when it asked about the contents and value.\nCallum Webb: I will make a new declaration. The destination is Canada, not the United States address saved in your account. Is that right?\nMoana Reed: Yes, Canada. The American address belongs to another relative. This parcel is going to Vancouver, and the postcode on the label is the Canadian one.\nCallum Webb: Now describe the main item in ordinary language. The form does not need a gift message. You mentioned clothing online; can we be more precise?\nMoana Reed: It is a wool scarf. I nearly wrote just gift, but the help note said to name the item, so wool scarf would be accurate.\nCallum Webb: The scale shows one point eight kilograms. That replaces your estimate of two and a half, which included a bag you are keeping.\nMoana Reed: Please record one point eight kilograms, then. The scarf cost sixty dollars and the puzzle was fifteen, so the total declared value is seventy-five New Zealand dollars.\nCallum Webb: I also need the sender address. The screen currently says fourteen Aroha Road, but the label on your box says fourteen Aroha Lane. Which is current?\nMoana Reed: Aroha Lane is correct. Road was my old address. The house number remains fourteen, which is probably why I missed the error.\nCallum Webb: Economy Track includes tracking and takes eight to twelve working days. Priority is faster but normally requires a weekday signature.\nMoana Reed: My cousin works away from home on weekdays, so the signature could cause difficulty. Economy Track is fine; I care more about being able to follow the parcel than about the shortest time.\nCallum Webb: Before payment, let us check every item against the current destination list. You have the scarf, a small wooden puzzle, a watercolour set and this jar of honey. The list on my screen says the honey cannot be accepted in this parcel.\nMoana Reed: Then I will take the honey out. I have posted it within New Zealand before, so I assumed it would be acceptable overseas as well.\nCallum Webb: Domestic acceptance does not prove international acceptance. The sender must confirm each item is permitted, although counter staff can help.\nMoana Reed: Understood. What about the wooden puzzle? It is painted and new, not an antique, and it is still in the shop packaging.\nCallum Webb: The puzzle and watercolours are acceptable today. Keep the descriptions specific so the declaration matches the contents.\nMoana Reed: Should I handwrite another address label? My writing is clear, but there is not much room beside the return address.\nCallum Webb: Do not add another handwritten label. The counter printer produces the final address and customs labels together. I will place them on the largest face of the box and leave the seams clear.\nMoana Reed: So the completed declaration says Canada, wool scarf, one point eight kilograms, seventy-five dollars and fourteen Aroha Lane. The honey is removed, and I have chosen Economy Track.\nCallum Webb: Exactly. Confirm the declaration, and I will print the labels today. Keep the tracking receipt, but remember that the sender remains responsible for an accurate contents statement.",
