      "transcript": "Zoe Bartlett: Welcome to Harbour Shelf Ocean Pool. I am Zoe, the duty lifeguard. Before anyone changes or enters the water, I will describe the site plan, today’s tide arrangements and the route we use when one area closes.\nZoe Bartlett: You are at the street entrance on the south edge, with north at the top. Reception is directly ahead in the centre.\nZoe Bartlett: Look at the plan. From the entrance, turn right before reception and follow the path to the eastern wall. The changing room is the long public room immediately south of the smaller staff store.\nZoe Bartlett: Return to reception and take the path on your left. Pass the ticket office nearest reception, then turn slightly north. The first-aid room is below the centre of the shallow end. Do not continue to the far western boundary.\nZoe Bartlett: From first aid, continue north-west along the boundary. The accessible ramp is directly below the shallow end, at the far western edge. Nearby steps are shorter, but only the ramp has a handrail and gradual slope.\nZoe Bartlett: The main lap lanes occupy the centre of the pool. The tide gate is beyond their northern end, just outside the upper-right corner of the lane area. Staff operate it; swimmers must never try to move it, even when the water appears calm.\nZoe Bartlett: Now look south-east of reception. Two possible places sit near the street entrance. The family deck is the more central one, with benches and a clear view of the shallow area. The other place, farther east near the boundary, is only a delivery bay.\nZoe Bartlett: Finally, the equipment racks are south-west of reception, closer to the street entrance than the first-aid room. Personal property belongs in lockers; the racks are for kickboards and pool wheelchairs.\nZoe Bartlett: You now have the main layout. We will move on to the safety details shown on the same plan.\nZoe Bartlett: This pool is affected by coastal conditions. Staff check the local forecast and the tide board throughout the day. A high tide does not close the pool automatically, but waves crossing the outer wall may change the decision.\nZoe Bartlett: If a section closes, staff raise a red flag beside reception. The electronic sign repeats the message, but the flag is the quickest answer if you are already on the deck. A yellow flag means extra caution, not closure.\nZoe Bartlett: Before walking to the northern path, read the tide board. It displays the staff inspection time as well as the expected tide. A phone forecast may be useful before travelling, but the tide board gives the decision for this facility.\nZoe Bartlett: At the accessible ramp, enter facing forward and use the handrail. The painted edge is a visual guide; it is not something to grip. If you need the pool wheelchair, ask at reception rather than moving one from the equipment rack yourself.\nZoe Bartlett: Children may watch from the family deck, but anyone entering the pool outside a coached lesson needs adult supervision. Lifeguards oversee the entire facility, so families must keep that supervision in place throughout the visit.\nZoe Bartlett: The lap lanes run north to south. Keep the ramp landing clear, and walk around the wide southern end to reach the far side.\nZoe Bartlett: If the tide gate closes during your visit, leave the northern deck by the signed route west of the lap lanes. Do not return through the gate area to collect belongings. Staff will recover anything left there when conditions allow.\nZoe Bartlett: After swimming, shower briefly, return borrowed equipment to the racks south-west of reception and use the changing room on the eastern wall. Please report any damaged handrail or loose surface immediately instead of assuming another visitor has already done so.\nZoe Bartlett: To recap: a red flag shows closure, read the tide board, use the ramp handrail, and remember that children require adult supervision. We will now walk the safe southern route.",
