      "title": "Listening — Part 3: Educational discussion: two trainee reporters verify a fictional breaking-news image in Toronto",
      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct source, A-G, for each check.\n26-30: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Simone Baptiste: This photograph reached our queue at eight fifteen. Its caption claims flooding outside a Toronto station this morning, but we must verify origin, time and location.\nDeclan Shore: It shows water across a road, a red bus and a shop with a blue awning. Those details do not prove the caption. Let us assign each check.\nSimone Baptiste: First, we need the earliest known online appearance. I will run a reverse-image search. If an older copy exists, the current post may be recycling an image from another event.\nDeclan Shore: For the camera time, I will inspect the image metadata. A timestamp can be altered or stripped, so it is a clue rather than proof.\nSimone Baptiste: The sky in the photograph is bright, yet the caption says heavy rain was falling at seven. We can compare that visual clue with a weather archive for the claimed place and hour.\nDeclan Shore: For location, the shop sign is blurred, but the mural and the shape of the junction are distinctive. A street map with recent imagery should let us compare those fixed landmarks.\nSimone Baptiste: I will contact the original uploader, ask what they saw and request the uncompressed file. We can also confirm permission.\nDeclan Shore: So: reverse search for earliest appearance, metadata for time, weather archive for conditions, street map for landmarks, and the uploader for first-hand context.\nSimone Baptiste: I have the reverse search result. The same photograph appeared eighteen months ago on a community blog in Halifax. It was cropped differently, but the bus, awning and water pattern are identical.\nDeclan Shore: Then it is not a new Toronto image. A current account has attached a fresh caption to an old photograph.\nSimone Baptiste: The metadata contains a creation date matching that older post, not today. However, the location field is empty. That supports the age finding but gives us no coordinates.\nDeclan Shore: Missing coordinates do not prove manipulation. Devices or platforms may omit them. The file does not establish location; that is all we can conclude.\nSimone Baptiste: The weather archive makes the Toronto caption even less likely. The claimed hour was dry and overcast, whereas the image has strong sunlight and deep shadows. Still, weather alone cannot identify Halifax.\nDeclan Shore: I compared the mural with street imagery. It matches a junction near Halifax Harbour, including the unusual triangular window above the shop. The red vehicle is a tour bus, not a Toronto transit bus as I first assumed.\nSimone Baptiste: The correction matters. Colour suggested public transport, but the visible company logo and route context are stronger evidence.\nDeclan Shore: The uploader has replied. She took the original during a coastal storm in Halifax and posted it to the community blog. She did not upload today’s copy and did not give the current account permission.\nSimone Baptiste: We now have converging evidence: an older publication, an old creation date, Halifax landmarks and the photographer’s account. The Toronto caption is false, although the underlying scene itself was real.\nDeclan Shore: Should we publish a correction immediately? The misleading post has only a small audience, and repeating the image might spread it further.\nSimone Baptiste: We should hold the image, preserve our evidence and alert the news editor. The editor can choose a correction, verification note or no article.\nDeclan Shore: We should not contact the transit authority because we know the scene is elsewhere. A source call should test a live uncertainty.\nSimone Baptiste: I will save screenshots with dates and URLs, plus the original file supplied by the photographer. I will record which search produced each result and when we contacted the uploader. A written trail lets another reporter reproduce our conclusion rather than trusting our memory.\nDeclan Shore: Our wording should separate findings from limits. The image predates today and depicts Halifax, but we cannot infer who wrote the caption or their motive.\nSimone Baptiste: Exactly. The strongest result is not simply fake image. It is a genuine older photograph presented with a false current location and time. That description is both more precise and easier to defend.\nDeclan Shore: I will update the log: do not publish as breaking news, refer the decision to the editor, retain the evidence and avoid claims about motive. We should also note that our conclusion depends on several independent checks, not one technical clue.",
