      "title": "Listening — Part 4: Academic monologue: cryoconite holes in a polar-science lecture at a university in Otago",
      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the sentences. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Today we will examine cryoconite holes, small melt features found on glacier surfaces. Their name refers to dark material on ice, but the holes are not merely dirty depressions. They connect physical melting, microbial activity and the movement of water across a glacier.\nLecturer: Cryoconite begins with fine mineral dust and organic particles deposited by wind, meltwater or nearby exposed ground. The material is visibly darker than clean snow or ice, and researchers compare the two surfaces carefully.\nLecturer: The relevant property is albedo, the proportion of incoming radiation a surface reflects. Clean bright ice has relatively high albedo, while a dark patch has lower albedo. We will return later to the physical consequence of that contrast.\nLecturer: Dark particles can settle into a shallow cavity while remaining exposed to light from above. Meltwater collects around them, and the wet sediment creates a small habitat whose depth and outline can change during the season.\nLecturer: The sediment is not biologically inert. Bacteria, algae and other microorganisms live among the grains. Sticky substances produced by microbes help bind fine particles into granules; the resulting structure is compact rather than a completely loose layer of dust.\nLecturer: Those granules provide surfaces, nutrients and small protected spaces. Photosynthetic organisms can add organic carbon, while other microbes consume or transform material. The community is shaped by both mineral particles and biological interactions.\nLecturer: Conditions differ among glaciers. On temperate ice, water may move through open surface channels. In very cold polar settings, a lid of ice can form above the liquid water for part of the season.\nLecturer: Researchers record the presence of that lid together with diameter, depth, sediment mass, light and temperature. These measurements allow apparently similar holes to be compared without assuming that they function in the same way.\nLecturer: Water routes also matter. Moving meltwater can carry cells, dissolved nutrients and fine sediment. A channel may add or remove material quickly, whereas a hole without a visible channel may retain a more local chemical signal.\nLecturer: Sampling creates a further difficulty because removing water or sediment changes the structure being measured. A scoop can mix layers, disturb granules and carry surrounding ice into the material collected for analysis.\nLecturer: That final problem is contamination. Good field practice records the location and physical form before collection, then uses clean tools. Care reduces introduced material, although natural differences between holes still remain.\nLecturer: We can now explain why the darker material may deepen the ice beneath it. Its lower albedo means that it absorbs more incoming energy than bright ice, so local melting can proceed more quickly.\nLecturer: This mechanism does not mean that every dark patch develops at the same rate. Sediment thickness, shade, weather and drainage all modify the response, so appearance alone is not enough to predict final depth. A thin patch may disperse, while a sheltered deposit can persist through several measurement visits.\nLecturer: The ice lid also needs careful interpretation. It can make a hole relatively closed for a period, but the system is not permanently sealed. Light can pass through ice, and later melting can reopen a connection.\nLecturer: Consequently, a covered hole may still change biologically and chemically. Researchers note when the lid forms, whether gases or water can move, and when seasonal drainage reconnects the feature to the wider glacier surface. Temperature records help distinguish a temporary cover from a longer winter condition.\nLecturer: Replicate samples are valuable because they represent spatial variation better than one hole. Several samples from a defined area reveal whether a result is common or simply a feature of one unusual cavity.\nLecturer: Repeating those samples through the melt season adds a time dimension. It can show whether changes in microbes, water chemistry or depth are stable patterns or brief responses to recent conditions. Consistent collection intervals make comparisons across visits easier to interpret. Repeated visits also show whether different areas follow comparable seasonal trajectories.\nLecturer: The strongest study design therefore combines biological sampling with physical and hydrological observations. Microbial groups and pigments are interpreted alongside depth, reflectance, water routes, temperature and the timing of lid formation.\nLecturer: Integration does not remove uncertainty. A relationship between darker sediment and deeper holes is consistent with enhanced absorption, but exposure time, weather and water movement can also affect depth. Mechanisms must be tested rather than assumed. Field notes should therefore record exceptions as carefully as the expected pattern.\nLecturer: To summarise, mineral dust begins the feature, microbes organize particles into granules, and local surface properties influence melting. Careful records, clean collection, repeated sampling and linked biological, physical and water measurements turn a small dark cavity into a useful scientific system.",
