      "part": 6,
      "skill": "reading",
      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 2: Ropewalks: Factories Shaped by Their Product",
      "passageTitle": "Ropewalks: Factories Shaped by Their Product",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Before mechanised factories compressed production into compact floors, rope was made along an extended path. Fibres had to be drawn out and twisted over a distance related to the intended cordage. The word ropewalk came to describe both this method and the specialised place where it occurred. A worker could move backwards while paying out hemp, with separate strands later combined into larger rope. The product therefore imposed an unusual geometry on the workplace. Instead of arranging many short operations around a central power source, builders provided a remarkably long, narrow route along which tension and alignment could be maintained. The route was therefore part of the production apparatus itself.\n\nB Length was not decorative. Historical ropewalks could extend for hundreds or even thousands of feet, and a building had to accommodate the rope being laid. Some operations used covered structures; others worked on open or partly sheltered walks. Local climate, investment and the value of continuous production influenced the choice, so surviving buildings should not be mistaken for a single universal type. Whatever the enclosure, the linear plan organised people, reels and moving strands. It also created practical difficulties: communication across distance, repeated walking, the movement of bulky fibre and the risk that one interruption would affect work along the whole line. Its proportions followed the work rather than a conventional room plan.\n\nC The Charlestown Navy Yard in Boston opened its ropewalk in 1838. The building followed what is now Chelsea Street for 1,325 feet and could produce rope up to roughly a quarter of a mile long. Initially powered by steam, it incorporated innovations associated with Daniel Treadwell. Granite and brick construction made it notable as an early attempt at fireproofing. The government site became the Navy's largest ropemaking facility, but it did not supply every requirement. Private firms provided most naval rope, while the public factory could expand output in wartime and also test or develop products. Its public value lay partly in this combination of production and experimentation.\n\nD That reserve role complicates a simple story of obsolete craft. Steamships still needed cordage even after sails ceased to dominate propulsion, and wartime demand could rise sharply. A battleship used rope for mooring, handling equipment and many routine operations. The Navy therefore valued a plant that retained specialised labour and machinery when private supply was slow to respond. After the Second World War, however, federal officials reconsidered which industries government should operate directly. Debate about closure concerned not only immediate output but the less visible value of testing capacity and technical knowledge. Keeping that capacity available was itself part of the institutional calculation.\n\nE At Jones Point near Alexandria, Virginia, Josiah Davis built a different ropewalk in 1833. It was a narrow, two-storey structure about 400 yards long, serving the maritime trade of the nearby port. A later recollection described boys running through the abandoned building and playing on a large reel, evidence of an industrial space acquiring informal uses after production ended. During operation, a spinner walked backwards between reels, unwinding hemp carried around the waist and forming strands by hand. The work joined bodily movement to the building's length; one account suggests a spinner might walk 20 miles during a ten-hour day. Here too, the working method and the architecture were inseparable.\n\nF Much of the Jones Point structure disappeared, so archaeology supplies evidence unavailable from standing architecture. Excavation exposed foundation remains and a black stain running along the centre. Investigators interpreted the stain as probably resulting from tar applied to cordage to resist decay in seawater. The mark is modest compared with an intact factory, yet it links a material trace to a production stage. Its interpretation also requires caution: the stain supports a probable explanation, not a complete reconstruction of every task, machine or worker who occupied the building. The evidence is strongest when trace, process and setting are considered together.\n\nG Ropewalks matter because form, labour and product are unusually legible together. Their extreme length records a manufacturing constraint, while walls, power systems and fire precautions reflect local decisions about risk and investment. Documentary sources can identify dates, managers or output; surviving structures reveal spatial organisation; archaeology may recover residues after walls have gone. None is sufficient alone. Combined, they show how an apparently simple object depended on coordinated motion, specialised infrastructure and maritime markets. Preservation therefore protects evidence of an industrial system, not merely an eccentric long building. Reading the traces together also exposes what disappears with machinery, routine and spoken knowledge. Dimensions cannot recover workshop discipline, but they establish the physical scale within which it operated. In that way, an elongated shell becomes evidence about labour, supply, technology and institutional choice across changing institutions.",
