      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Paramedics are usually imagined responding to unpredictable emergencies and transporting patients to hospital. Community paramedicine broadens that model. Some programmes schedule home visits, receive referrals from primary care or follow people who repeatedly call emergency services. During a visit, a practitioner may assess health, review medicines, provide education or connect a patient with another service. The purpose is not simply to replace an ambulance journey with a cheaper journey. It is to intervene earlier, improve access and decide which setting can meet a need safely. Because programmes differ, the label names a family of arrangements rather than one standard intervention. Designs therefore take different routes to earlier care.\n\nB The diversity is substantial. A systematic review identified programmes serving frequent emergency callers, people at risk of hospital admission, older adults and other groups. Most described home visits, yet their services and training varied. Some retained a strong acute-care emphasis; others added prevention, screening, counselling or referral. Such variation may be useful because local gaps differ. It also obstructs simple comparison. If one evaluation measures emergency calls and another measures medication adherence, a combined claim about effectiveness can conceal unlike objectives. Before asking whether community paramedicine works, analysts must specify for whom, through which activities and against what alternative. Comparison must keep those intended endpoints visible.\n\nC Referral pathways reveal the institutional change involved. A general practitioner may identify a patient suitable for a home visit, while a paramedic may discover needs requiring primary care, social support or urgent transport. Information must travel in both directions, and responsibility cannot remain vague when a condition worsens. Scope of practice therefore concerns more than a list of clinical procedures. It includes access to records, supervision, escalation rules and the authority to make or receive referrals. Programmes that add tasks without clarifying these boundaries may create another handoff rather than genuine continuity. Continuity also depends on that institutional wiring.\n\nD Training presents a parallel problem. The systematic review found that many programme reports described training incompletely, and reported content ranged from acute assessment to communication, leadership and knowledge of community services. Existing paramedic education supplies relevant foundations, but planned care can require different habits from rapid emergency response. A home visit may expose medication routines, housing hazards or social isolation that cannot be addressed by a single technical intervention. A useful framework would identify core competencies while allowing additional preparation for the population and partners in each programme. Standardisation should establish a floor, not erase local adaptation. Shared standards and local preparation serve different needs.\n\nE Advocates often expect planned visits to reduce pressure on emergency departments. That outcome is plausible, but substitution must be demonstrated. A programme may reduce ambulance transport for enrolled patients while creating new appointments, referrals or follow-up work elsewhere. This additional activity can still be valuable if it meets neglected needs or prevents later harm. It simply means that success cannot be inferred from one falling count. Evaluators should trace changes across services and distinguish avoided emergency use from care that was shifted, delayed or newly provided. A broader service ledger distinguishes these possibilities.\n\nF Early programme reports illustrate both promise and risk. One ambulance trust described teams integrated with local general practices, undertaking some home visits while continuing to answer emergency calls. Its annual report presented encouraging early figures from a particular pilot. Those observations can justify further testing, but they cannot establish that the same results will occur in every region. Participants were selected through local pathways, the comparison period was limited, and surrounding services mattered. Treating an operational report as universal proof would confuse evidence that a model can function with evidence about its average effect. The report remains informative within local context.\n\nG System incentives also shape the model. Ambulance organisations are commonly measured through response and transport indicators, whereas planned prevention may produce benefits outside those categories or after a long delay. Funding tied only to emergency activity can therefore make successful prevention look like lost work. Conversely, paying for home visits alone can reward volume without showing benefit. Commissioners need measures connected to the programme's stated mechanism: access, safe treatment, patient experience, subsequent service use and appropriate escalation. They should also monitor unintended consequences, including missed deterioration, duplicated assessment and unequal enrolment. Balanced incentives need activity and outcome measures.\n\nH The strongest case for community paramedicine is conditional. Paramedics can bring assessment skills and mobile access into homes, and may connect patients with services before a crisis. Yet an expanded role does not itself prove an outcome. A credible programme defines its target group, referral pathway, clinical boundaries, training and theory of change before selecting measures. Evaluation then asks whether care improved, replaced other activity or merely added to it, and whether gains were fair. This avoids treating every prevented journey as success or dismissing local innovation. Community paramedicine should be judged as a designed system, not as a job title with assumed effects. Evidence should follow the whole pathway so access gains can be distinguished from activity shifted between organisations, and local dependencies from mechanisms that may travel more reliably. It also keeps unenrolled patients and unmet needs visible.",
